    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every figure attached to the Wellfit TM037 comes from the people selling it. That is
          normal for a budget walking pad, and it does not make the machine a bad one. It does mean
          the useful review is not a list of features but a reading of the listing: which numbers
          describe the product, which describe the marketing, and which one independent report has
          already called into question. This page does that reading, and then says plainly who
          the TM037 is for.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is the Wellfit Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            The Wellfit TM037 is a budget walking pad, and judged as one it is a plausible purchase.
            It is cheap, light, folds flat and is listed with a handrail that lets you use it either
            under a desk or as a small standalone walker. For someone who wants to walk slowly for an
            hour or two a day while working, in a small flat, on a budget of a couple of hundred
            dollars, it does roughly what that buyer needs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The caveats are specific. The motor is advertised as 3.0 HP peak and brushless, and one
            independent reviewer has reported a teardown that found a brushed permanent magnet DC
            motor with a continuous rating of roughly 0.75 HP. We have not verified that, but it is
            consistent with what a peak figure on a walking pad usually conceals. The warranty
            wording differs between the brand page and the Amazon listing, and the effective cover on
            the motor appears to be one year. And owner complaints about returns, belt shifting and
            early motor failure have been documented in one independent review.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the honest answer is conditional. Buy it for light walking, at a street price toward
            the bottom of its range, from a seller whose return window you have read, with the
            warranty terms in writing. Do not buy it to run on, to carry a heavy user every day for
            hours, or in the expectation of long manufacturer support. The rest of this page explains
            why each of those conditions is there.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
