              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the TM037 Actually Is</h2>
<p>
            Wellfit sells a small range of walking pads, and the TM037 — sometimes written TM-037 —
            is the one most "Wellfit treadmill" and "Wellfit walking pad" searches end up on. It is a
            two-in-one design: a low, flat walking deck with a front handrail that folds up for
            standalone walking and folds down so the deck can sit under a standing desk.
          </p>
          <p>
            That format matters more than any single number on the listing. A walking pad is not a
            small treadmill in the way most people picture one. There is no tall upright, no console
            column, no motorised incline and usually no side rails. The motor and belt sit in a
            shallow housing a few inches off the floor, and the whole machine is designed to be slid
            under a sofa or stood on its end against a wall when you are not using it. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill comparison</a>
            sets out what that format gives up and what it gains.
          </p>
          <p>
            The seller lists the TM037 at about 42 lb (41.7 lb), with a footprint of about 43.3 by
            21.3 inches and a height of roughly 5 inches with the handrail down. It is sold about 95%
            pre-assembled, which in practice means unboxing it and raising the handrail rather than
            building anything. Those figures are the reason people buy it: it is light enough for one
            person to move and small enough to disappear.
          </p>
          <p>
            Price is the other reason. At the time of writing the TM037 typically sells for roughly
            $140 to $260 on marketplaces, depending on the seller and the day, while the brand site
            shows a higher "regular" price. Treat the regular price as a reference point rather than
            a guide to value. At the bottom of that street-price range the machine is cheap enough
            that the risks discussed below are proportionate; toward the top of it, the comparison
            with better-supported walking pads becomes closer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="listed-specifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Listed Specifications, and How to Read Them</h2>
<p>
            The Wellfit brand page and the Amazon listing agree on the core figures. That agreement
            tells you the seller is consistent; it does not tell you the figures have been checked by
            anyone else. The table below lists each claim alongside how we treat it on this page.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Item</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Seller's listing</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">36 x 16 in, five-layer cushioned, auto-alignment</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6-6.2 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0-5%, manual only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">320 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Machine weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 42 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Listing claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.0 HP peak, quiet brushless</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Disputed by one teardown report</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Noise</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under 40 dB</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unverified manufacturer claim</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Differs between brand page and Amazon</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflicting</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A 36 by 16-inch belt is a walking belt</h3>
          <p>
            Thirty-six inches of belt length is enough for a walking stride at desk pace and not much
            more. Sixteen inches of width leaves little room for the sideways drift in foot placement
            that creeps in when your attention is on a screen rather than on your feet, which is
            exactly the situation an under-desk pad is bought for. Neither figure is unusual for a
            walking pad; both are far short of what a running stride needs. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through stride length against belt length, and the short version is that this belt
            is for walking.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A 6.2 mph ceiling means brisk walking and a light jog at most</h3>
          <p>
            The listed range runs from 0.6 to 6.2 mph. Most desk walking happens at the very bottom
            of that, often below 2 mph, because typing and talking get harder as pace rises. The top
            end is a brisk walk for most adults and a light jog for some. Treat any jogging as
            occasional and short, with the handrail up — on many two-in-one pads the higher speeds are
            intended for handrail-up use, so check what the manual says for this one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Manual incline means stepping off to change it</h3>
          <p>
            The 0-5% incline is listed as manual only. There is no motor to raise the deck mid-walk;
            you set it by hand, with the belt stopped and you off the deck, following the method in
            the manual. That is fine if you pick one angle and leave it. It rules out incline
            intervals and any workout that changes gradient as you go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Motor Question: Peak Horsepower, Brushless Claims and One Teardown</h2>
<p>
            The TM037 is advertised with a 3.0 HP peak motor, described as quiet and brushless. Both
            parts of that description deserve a closer look, and one independent report has
            challenged both.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What a peak figure does and does not tell you</h3>
          <p>
            Treadmill motors are rated in two different ways. A continuous rating (often written CHP)
            describes what the motor can deliver for as long as you keep walking. A peak rating
            describes a brief maximum the motor can reach under load before heat or the controller
            pulls it back. Peak figures are always the larger of the two, sometimes by a wide margin,
            and they are the figure budget machines tend to print. A peak number on its own cannot be
            compared with a continuous number on another machine, and it says little about how the
            motor behaves during the long, steady, low-speed sessions a walking pad actually spends
            its life doing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the distinction in full.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the independent teardown reported</h3>
          <p>
            One independent reviewer has reported opening a TM037 and finding a motor label that read
            "permanent magnet DC", on a motor with a two-wire connection. The reviewer read that as a
            brushed motor, which would contradict the brushless claim on the listing. From the
            voltage and current printed on the same label, the reviewer derived a continuous rating
            of roughly 0.75 HP.
          </p>
          <p>
            We have not verified this. It is one report, from one unit, and manufacturers of budget
            products do change components between production batches without changing the model
            name. It may not describe the unit you receive. But it is specific, it is based on a
            label rather than an opinion, and it fits a pattern common in the category, where a large
            peak figure sits in front of a modest continuous one. We think a buyer should know about
            it, weigh it as one data point, and not treat the 3.0 HP figure as a measure of
            continuous output.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why it matters for durability and noise</h3>
          <p>
            Brushed and brushless are both DC designs, and both are found in home treadmills. The
            practical differences are wear and sound. Brushed motors have carbon brushes that wear
            and eventually need replacing, and brush contact adds a source of sound;
            brushless motors avoid that wear item. Our
            <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC versus DC motor guide</a>
            covers where each sits, and
            <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the motor brushes guide</a>
            covers what brush wear looks like.
          </p>
          <p>
            Continuous rating matters for heat. A motor working close to its continuous limit for
            hours at a time — a heavier user, a long desk session, a dry belt adding friction — runs
            hotter, and heat is what shortens motor and controller life. If the teardown figure is
            representative, the TM037 has less headroom than its listing implies, which makes light
            use and good belt maintenance more important rather than less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Reading the 320 lb Capacity Claim Sensibly</h2>
<p>
            The seller claims a 320 lb maximum user weight. That figure appears on a machine listed
            at about 42 lb, with a low-profile deck a few inches thick. It is worth thinking about
            what the number can and cannot mean in that context.
          </p>
          <p>
            A weight capacity is a summary of the whole system: frame, deck, rollers, belt and motor
            together. On a full-size treadmill a high capacity usually comes with a heavy frame, a
            thick deck and a motor with real continuous headroom, and the machine weight reflects it.
            On a walking pad, a high claimed capacity sits on a much lighter structure, which means
            there is less mass to absorb each footfall and less margin in every component that
            carries the load. The figure may be accurate for the test the seller ran. It is not the
            same thing as a machine built for a heavy user to walk on every day for hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            The motor question compounds this. Body weight is the single biggest load on a walking
            pad motor at low speed, because the motor has to keep the belt moving against the
            friction of a person standing on it. If the continuous rating is closer to the teardown
            report than to the peak figure on the listing, a user near the claimed limit is asking a
            lot of it, for long periods, at the speeds where cooling is least effective.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our general rule is to buy a machine whose rated capacity sits comfortably above your
            body weight — around a quarter above is the margin we suggest — and to treat that rule
            more conservatively still on a light walking pad. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains what the rating does and does not cover. If you are near or above the upper end
            of the TM037's claim, or plan long daily sessions, the machines in
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our guide for heavier users</a>
            are the better place to start, even though they cost more and take up more room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Noise: The Under-40 dB Claim</h2>
<p>
            The listing describes the TM037 as quiet and puts its noise below 40 dB. That figure is a
            manufacturer claim and we have not seen it independently verified. It also does not say
            how or where it was measured: at what speed, with what load on the belt, at what
            distance, and whether it covers the motor alone or the whole machine with a person
            walking on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those details matter because the motor is often not the loudest thing about a walking
            pad. Footfall is. Each step sends a small impact into the deck and through it into the
            floor, and on a suspended timber floor or in an upstairs flat that thud carries further
            than motor hum does. Belt slap, a roller that needs attention or a belt running dry can
            all add noise that no motor specification accounts for. If the teardown report about the
            motor type is right, brushed-motor noise would be part of the picture too.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical approach is to plan for normal walking-pad noise and treat anything quieter
            as a bonus. A dense mat under the machine helps with footfall transmission, a lubricated
            belt helps with friction noise, and walking with a lighter step helps more than most
            people expect. If noise is the deciding factor — a shared office, a neighbour below, a
            sleeping household — read our
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmill guide</a>
            first; it separates motor noise from structural noise and explains what genuinely
            reduces each. Our page on whether
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">you need a treadmill mat</a>
            covers the floor side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apps-and-controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Controls, Apps and What They Cost</h2>
<p>
            The seller lists four ways to control the TM037: buttons on the handlebar, a handheld
            remote, an app and voice control. For under-desk use the remote is the one most owners
            will actually rely on, because the handrail is folded down and the deck is under the
            desk, out of reach. Keep the remote somewhere fixed; a walking pad with a lost remote and
            the handrail down is awkward to stop quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four app integrations are listed: the Wellfit app, Kinomap, Zwift and Apple Health. The
            first is the brand's own companion app. Apple Health is a place for your walking data to
            end up rather than something that controls the machine. Kinomap and Zwift are
            third-party training platforms, each a separate service with its own account and its
            own membership terms — check the current price with each before counting it as part of
            the deal. None of them is needed to use the walking pad itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. First, app support on budget machines can change over time as companion
            apps are updated or retired, so buy the TM037 for what it does with the remote and the
            handrail, and treat app features as extras. Second, how well a training platform reads
            speed and distance from a particular walking pad varies, and a listing that names an app
            is not a guarantee of a smooth experience with it. Our
            <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            explains how treadmill connections to Zwift generally work and what to check before you
            rely on one.
          </p>
          <p>
            For under-desk walking, most of this is secondary anyway. The step count from a watch or
            phone, a steady low speed and a remote within reach cover what most desk walkers want.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-and-service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Warranty, Returns and Seller Service</h2>
<p>
            This is the section that should decide whether you buy, because on a machine at this
            price the support behind it matters as much as the hardware.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The warranty terms conflict</h3>
          <p>
            The Amazon listing claims three years of cover on the frame and one year on the motor and
            parts. The Wellfit brand page says "1-Year Warranty". Those two statements are not
            obviously compatible, and the brand page is the more conservative of them. On either
            reading, the motor and parts — the components most likely to fail on a walking pad — are
            covered for one year. That is the figure we suggest you plan around. Frame cover on a
            low-profile walking pad is worth relatively little, because frames are rarely what fails.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before you buy, get the warranty terms in writing from the seller you are buying from:
            the length, what it covers, whether it covers labour or parts only, who pays to ship a
            faulty unit back, and how a claim is made. A screenshot of a listing is better than
            nothing; a written reply from the seller is better still. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            lists the questions to ask.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What owners have reported</h3>
          <p>
            One independent review documents owner complaints about the TM037 and its seller: returns
            that were difficult to complete, a motor failure at around five to six months of use, and
            belts that shifted to one side. These are reported experiences rather than established
            facts, and every budget walking pad attracts some complaints of this kind. But the
            pattern lines up with the motor question above, and a motor failing inside the first year
            is exactly the scenario in which the warranty and the seller's responsiveness are tested.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Check the return window before you order</h3>
          <p>
            Return terms for walking pads vary by seller and by marketplace, and they can change. Read
            the current return window, find out whether opened items are accepted, and check who pays
            return shipping on a 42 lb box. Then use the window: walk on the machine properly in the
            first days, at the speeds and for the session lengths you actually intend, so that any
            problem shows up while returning it is still simple. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmill return policies</a>
            covers what to look for, and
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explains how marketplace sellers and fulfilment affect who handles a return.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="desk-use-and-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Daily Under-Desk Use and Keeping It Running</h2>
<p>
            Most TM037 owners will use it the same way: handrail down, deck under a standing desk,
            walking slowly for long stretches while working. That use is gentle on the legs and
            surprisingly demanding on the machine, because hours of continuous running at low speed
            produce more heat and belt wear than a short brisk session does. A few habits make a
            meaningful difference to how long a walking pad lasts.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Break long sessions up</h3>
          <p>
            Check the manual for any recommended maximum session length or rest period. Where none is
            given, stopping the belt for a few minutes between long sessions is a sensible precaution
            on any budget motor, and especially on one whose continuous rating is in question.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Lubricate the belt on schedule</h3>
          <p>
            A dry belt increases friction between belt and deck, which increases the load on the
            motor and the heat it produces. On a walking pad that is the most common avoidable cause
            of early motor and belt problems. Follow the manual for the lubricant type and interval;
            our <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication walkthrough</a>
            covers the method, which on a low-profile pad usually means lifting the belt edge and
            applying lubricant under it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Watch belt tracking</h3>
          <p>
            The listing describes an auto-alignment belt, and belt shifting is one of the complaints
            reported by owners. Whatever the design, walking pads drift: you tend to walk slightly off
            centre at a desk, and the belt follows. If the belt creeps toward one side, correct it
            early with small adjustments at the rear roller bolts, as the manual describes, rather
            than letting it rub against the housing.
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">How to centre a treadmill belt</a>
            walks through the adjustment step by step.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Safety basics</h3>
          <p>
            Unplug the machine before any adjustment, lubrication or cleaning. Stop using it and
            contact the seller if you notice a burning smell, if it trips a breaker on start-up, if
            the speed surges on its own, or if the belt does not stop when you use the stop control.
            Keep children and pets away from a walking pad that is running or plugged in, and keep
            the remote within reach. The manual is the authority on this model; where it differs from
            general advice, follow the manual.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who It Suits, and Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>It suits light daily walking at a desk.</strong> If the plan is to walk slowly
            while working for an hour or two a day, the TM037's format, weight and size fit that well,
            and its price makes the experiment cheap. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk guide</a>
            covers desk height and setup.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits small spaces.</strong> A machine listed at about 42 lb and roughly 5
            inches tall can live under a bed or sofa and come out when needed, which is the main
            practical advantage of any walking pad over a folding treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits a tight budget.</strong> At the lower end of its street-price range it is
            cheap enough that a year of light walking represents reasonable value even if the machine
            does not last much beyond its motor warranty. That is a legitimate way to buy a product,
            as long as you go in knowing it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Runners should buy something else.</strong> A 36 by 16-inch belt and a 6.2 mph
            ceiling are walking specifications. If you want to run, you need a full-size treadmill,
            and our
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill comparison</a>
            sets out where the line falls.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heavy daily use calls for more machine.</strong> Several hours a day, every day,
            or a user near the claimed capacity, puts the TM037 in the situation where its disputed
            continuous rating matters most. A better-supported walking pad from our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>,
            or a full-size machine, is the safer choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Anyone who wants a long warranty should look elsewhere.</strong> One year of
            effective motor cover is short, and the conflicting terms add uncertainty. If long-term
            support matters to you, it is worth paying more for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are still choosing between pads, our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            explains which specifications to trust and which to discount, and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our under-$500 guide</a>
            shows what else this budget buys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Wellfit walking pad worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, for light daily walking at a desk on a tight budget. It is cheap, light and folds flat. The caveats are a motor whose brushless, 3.0 HP peak claim has been disputed by one independent teardown, effective motor cover of one year, and reported complaints about returns. Buy near the bottom of its price range with the warranty and return terms confirmed in writing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on the Wellfit TM037?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any real sense. The listed belt is 36 by 16 inches and the top speed is 6.2 mph, which together make it a walking machine. Some users may manage a short, light jog at the top speed with the handrail up, but a running stride needs a longer and wider belt and more speed. Runners should look at a full-size treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Wellfit TM037 motor really brushless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The listing says it is, and advertises 3.0 HP peak. One independent reviewer reported a teardown in which the motor label read permanent magnet DC on a two-wire motor, which they read as brushed, and derived a continuous rating of roughly 0.75 HP. We have not verified that report, and units can vary between batches, but the 3.0 HP figure should not be read as continuous output.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the Wellfit TM037?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The seller claims 320 lb. That is a listing claim on a machine weighing about 42 lb, and it is not the same as a full-size machine built for heavy daily use. If you are close to that figure or plan long sessions every day, leave a generous margin and consider a heavier-built treadmill, especially given the questions about the motor's continuous rating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty does the Wellfit TM037 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The sources disagree. The Amazon listing claims three years on the frame and one year on the motor and parts, while the Wellfit brand page says 1-Year Warranty. Either way, the motor and parts are covered for about a year, so plan around that. Ask the seller you are buying from for the terms in writing, including who pays shipping on a claim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Wellfit TM037 quiet enough for an office or flat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The listing claims under 40 dB, but that figure is unverified and does not say how it was measured. On walking pads, footfall travelling into the floor is often louder than the motor, especially upstairs. A dense mat, a lubricated belt and a light step all help. If noise is critical, try it within the return window at your real walking speed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Wellfit TM037</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointment with budget walking pads comes from reading the listing as a specification sheet. These are the four errors that matter most with this one.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 3.0 HP peak as the motor size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A peak figure describes a brief maximum, not what the motor sustains for an hour of desk walking. One independent teardown reported a continuous rating of roughly 0.75 HP on this model. Whether or not that applies to your unit, compare walking pads on continuous ratings where they are given, and treat a listing that gives only a peak figure as one that has not told you the number that matters.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the longest warranty quoted is the one you get</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Amazon listing and the brand page describe different terms, and the motor and parts are covered for about a year on either. Buyers who remember the three-year figure are often surprised to find it applies to the frame, which rarely fails. Ask the seller for the terms in writing before ordering, and keep the reply with your proof of purchase.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the return window unused</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Reported problems with this machine — belt shifting and early motor trouble among them — tend to show up under real use, not in a five-minute test. Walk on it at your normal speed for your normal session length in the first days. If something is wrong, you want to find it while the return is straightforward rather than after the window closes and you depend on a warranty claim.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping belt lubrication because it is a walking pad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Low-profile pads still need lubricant between belt and deck, and long slow desk sessions make friction more important, not less. A dry belt raises motor load and heat, which on a budget motor shortens its life. Check the manual for the interval and lubricant type, set a reminder, and correct belt drift early rather than letting it rub against the housing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

